  "narrative": "No assessments are due, so this explore stage registers mechanistic refinements and robustness tests rather than validating the already accepted large signals. I keep the current scientific interpretation as supplied-dataset association, not causality or clinical generalizability. The approximate outcome-specific 10%-of-absolute-mean clinical scales remain: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, NFE2L2 and USP7 about 0.009, MDM2 about 0.027, KIF18A about 0.026, EGFR about 0.025, ERBB2 about 0.026, and KRAS about 0.0245. For direction -1 claims, a positive returned signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive returned signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed group.\n\nThe highest-priority unresolved signal is NF1 loss in KRAS-wildtype cells being associated with weaker KRAS dependency. H91/R61 is directionally clinically suggestive but imprecise enough that the lower bound falls below the KRAS clinical threshold. I therefore register two targeted NF1-loss refinements with available motivating evidence. First, I test NF1 loss only in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma cells, the dominant and biologically relevant subtype, to ask whether the bypass signal is carried by adenocarcinoma context. Second, I test NF1 loss in KRAS-wildtype, BRAF-wildtype, high-Cas9 screens to ask whether the signal persists after excluding another canonical MAPK-pathway bypass and poor Cas9 activity. If either remains clinically positive with lower bounds near or above the KRAS threshold, NF1-loss bypass biology becomes a candidate for targeted independent validation. If they attenuate, H91 should remain a promising but low-confidence dataset-specific signal.\n\nI also register high-Cas9 refinements for canonical driver hypotheses that are currently imprecise. KRAS-mutation-associated KRAS dependency overall is directionally consistent but below robust acceptance; restricting to higher Cas9 activity tests whether the true driver effect was diluted by weak screens. EGFR-amplification-associated EGFR dependency is borderline overall and is also tested overall in high-Cas9 screens and in a cleaner KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 context. The alternatives remain biologically plausible: EGFR amplification may confer stronger EGFR dependency, or KRAS co-occurrence and weak screens may mask a cleaner effect.\n\nFor RTK and bypass-pathway hypotheses, I register ERBB2-amplification effects in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 cells, PIK3CA-mutation effects on EGFR dependency and KRAS dependency in the same clean context, and the reverse-direction PTEN-loss stronger-EGFR-dependency signal in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 cells. These test whether PI3K or RTK pathway architecture shifts dependency from EGFR/KRAS or, for PTEN loss, whether the observed negative H93 signal reflected the opposite relationship. These are exploratory context tests, not causal pathway claims.\n\nFinally, I register TP53-loss effects on MDM2 dependency restricted to KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma. The overall TP53-loss weaker-MDM2 hypothesis H6 was near null, but MDM2 dependency is most interpretable in p53-pathway-relevant contexts; a cleaner driver context could reveal a biologically meaningful attenuation. I do not request validation at this stage because the key question is whether these refinements become both precise and clinically meaningful before spending validation budget.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 6 explore update. No assessments due. The accepted dataset-specific headline signals remain: SMARCA4 loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss weaker USP7 dependency. They are internally robust across high-Cas9 and adenocarcinoma strata and partially independently validated. This iteration registers mechanistic refinements linked to available evidence where possible. Priority: NF1-loss KRAS-wildtype refinements, including adenocarcinoma context and KRAS/BRAF-wildtype high-Cas9 context. Secondary: high-Cas9 refinements for KRAS mutation and EGFR amplification; cleaner KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 tests for EGFR amp, ERBB2 amp, PIK3CA effects on EGFR/KRAS, and reverse PTEN-loss stronger EGFR dependency; TP53-loss MDM2 in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma. No validation requested yet; reserve validation for a new driver/specificity effect that is both precise and clinically meaningful."
